|---|---|---|---|
| 14-0add-8deg-120s-N2-1250s (run1) | 103.6 | 0.75 | minor creases |
| 14p8-0add-17deg-530s-N2-1425s (run2) | 216.95 | 0.82 | wrinkles |
| 15-0add-9deg-250s-N2-1600s (run1) | 246.64 | 0.77 | clean |
| 15-0add-9deg-250s-N2-1600s (run4) | 221.34 | 0.75 | top gap |
| 15-0add-9deg-250s-N2-1600s (run8) | 211.06 | 0.68 | clean |
| 14p5-0add-10deg-200s-N2-1500s (run1) | 222.32 | 0.67 | clean |
| 14p5-0add-10deg-200s-N2-1500s (run2) | 209.06 | 0.69 | clean |
| 14p5-0add-10deg-200s-N2-1500s (run3) | 255.47 | 0.66 | top gap |
| 14p5-0add-10deg-200s-N2-1500s (run4) | 269.49 | 0.65 | clean |
| 17-0add-13deg-100s-N2-1800s (run2) | 354.35 | 0.61 | coverage gaps |

The **15-0add-9deg-250s-N2-1600s** condition (polymer_wt=15, bath_temp=9, mixing_temp=40, pullcast_speed=6, coupon_to_bath_wait_time=250, nips_bath_wait_time=1600, N2=true) stands out as a strong Pareto candidate: run1 gave modulus=246.64 with pore_fraction=0.77 (clean membrane), and run4 gave 221.34/0.75. However, run1 had a very high modulus SD (145.97 bar, CV high), and runs 2, 3, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 show substantial variability (pore fraction ranging 0.50–0.85, modulus 198–315 bar). This condition has been run 10 times with inconsistent results — the high-pore-fraction runs are not reproducibly confirmed.

**run5** gave pore_fraction=0.85 (highest observed) with modulus=276.81 — but had a top-gap coverage defect. **run8** gave pore_fraction=0.68, modulus=211 with a clean membrane. The scatter is large.

### Validation Status

The 15-0add-9deg-250s-N2-1600s condition has been run 10 times but shows high run-to-run variability in both objectives. No stable confirmed mean has emerged. The 14p8-0add-17deg-530s-N2-1425s run2 showed pore_fraction=0.82 but had wrinkles; run3 gave 0.63 with a wrinkle; run4 gave 0.44 — also highly variable.

### Exploration Opportunity

The data suggest that **lower polymer concentration (14–15 wt%) + cold bath (8–10°C) + moderate wait time (200–300s) + N2** is a promising region for high pore fraction. The 14p5-0add-10deg-200s-N2-1500s condition (runs 1–4) shows consistently good pore fraction (0.65–0.69) with modulus 209–269 bar and mostly clean membranes — this is a more reproducible candidate than 15-0add-9deg.

However, I notice an under-explored region: **slightly lower bath temperature (6–7°C) with polymer_wt ~14–14.5 and moderate wait time**. The 14p5-0add-7deg-200s-N2-1600s experiment gave pore_fraction=0.62, modulus=215 — decent but only one run. A colder bath should drive faster phase separation and potentially higher pore fraction while maintaining reasonable modulus.

Given the diversity context, **bath_temp=6°C** has not been tried. The region around polymer_wt=14.5, bath_temp=6, mixing_temp=40, moderate wait time is unexplored and physically motivated: colder bath → faster NIPS → more open structure (higher pore fraction), while 14.5 wt% polymer provides enough mechanical integrity for reasonable modulus.

This is a new exploration point rather than a repeat, justified because:
1. The 15-0add-9deg condition has been extensively repeated without convergence
2. The 14p5-0add-7deg condition showed promise in one run
3. bath_temp=6°C is genuinely unexplored
4. The combination targets both objectives simultaneously

```json
{
  "mixing_temp": 40,
  "bath_temp": 6,
  "pullcast_speed": 6,
  "nitrogen": true,
  "coupon_to_bath_wait_time": 200,
  "nips_bath_wait_time": 1600,
  "polymer_wt": 14.5,
  "additive_wt": 0
}
```

**Rationale:** Bath temperature of 6°C (unexplored) is colder than the 7–9°C range that has produced high pore fractions, which should drive faster non-solvent ingress and potentially higher pore fraction. Polymer_wt=14.5 wt% is in the range that has shown good pore fractions (0.62–0.69) without sacrificing too much modulus. Mixing_temp=40°C ensures proper dissolution. Pullcast_speed=6 mm/s and wait_time=200s are in the productive range for this polymer concentration. N2 blanket reduces humidity effects. This experiment will clarify whether further bath cooling beyond 7°C improves pore fraction while maintaining modulus, helping map the cold-bath frontier of the Pareto front.

